---
import ContactCTA from '../components/ContactCTA.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="PhD Research Proposal | Daniele Sala"
	description="PhD project plan: Forest-Water Relations During Drought. Isotopic and micrometeorological quantification of minor water pathways in stand-scale forest water budgets. ForFuS Doctoral Training Unit, Luxembourg Institute of Science and Technology."
>
	<div class="stack gap-20">
		<main class="wrapper stack gap-10">
			<Hero
				title="PhD Research Proposal"
				tagline="Forest-Water Relations During Drought: Isotopic and Micrometeorological Quantification of Minor Water Pathways in Stand-Scale Forest Water Budgets"
				align="start"
			/>

			<div class="meta-bar">
				<span class="meta-item"><strong>Programme:</strong> ForFuS Doctoral Training Unit · LIST (Luxembourg)</span>
				<span class="meta-item"><strong>Duration:</strong> 48 months</span>
				<span class="meta-item"><strong>Date:</strong> June 2026</span>
			</div>

			<div class="proposal-content">

				<!-- ABSTRACT -->
				<section class="abstract-box">
					<p>
						Forest water budgets are poorly constrained at the process level during drought: current
						evapotranspiration partitioning frameworks assign the dominant flux to canopy transpiration
						and leave forest floor evaporation as a residual absorbed by measurement error, while foliar
						water uptake (the direct absorption of water through leaf surfaces during fog, dew, or
						wetting events) remains essentially absent from operational models. This PhD will quantify
						these under-constrained water pathways using stable isotopic tracers (δ¹⁸O, δ²H) and
						sub-canopy micrometeorological monitoring across experimental forest stands in Luxembourg,
						Switzerland, and the Netherlands, and integrate the resulting process understanding into a
						stand-scale ecohydrological modelling framework within the ForFuS interdisciplinary doctoral
						training unit at the Luxembourg Institute of Science and Technology (LIST).
					</p>
				</section>

				<!-- PROBLEM STATEMENT -->
				<section>
					<h2>1. Problem Statement and Motivation</h2>
					<p>
						The dominant evapotranspiration partitioning frameworks used in forest hydrology,
						<strong>Penman-Monteith</strong> and <strong>Shuttleworth-Wallace</strong>, rest on the
						assumption that canopy transpiration accounts for the majority of the forest water flux.
						Forest floor evaporation, the evaporation from litter, moss, and bare soil beneath the
						canopy, is routinely treated as a minor residual. In practice, stand-level eddy covariance
						datasets frequently exhibit energy balance non-closure on the order of 10–30% of net
						radiation, and forest floor evaporation is precisely the term most likely to be subsumed
						into this residual error. The result is that one of the pathways most sensitive to
						drought-induced canopy thinning and understorey change is also the least well constrained
						observationally.
					</p>
					<p>
						Foliar water uptake (direct leaf-surface absorption during fog, dew, or rain-on-drought
						events) adds a further dimension that existing measurement frameworks cannot resolve
						independently. During severe water stress, when soil water potential drops below a critical
						threshold, the hydraulic gradient along the soil-plant-atmosphere continuum can reverse at
						the leaf-atmosphere interface, making the canopy a transient net water importer. Neither
						<strong>sap flow sensors</strong> (which measure xylem water flux, not foliar absorption)
						nor <strong>above-canopy eddy covariance</strong> (which integrates over the entire
						canopy-atmosphere interface) can isolate this pathway in isolation. Stable water isotopes
						offer a tracer-based route around this measurement barrier, but their application to
						distinguishing foliar uptake from transpiration remains methodologically contested.
					</p>
					<p>
						The frequency and severity of drought events in central Europe has increased markedly in
						recent decades, with documented consequences for forest water status and mortality risk
						[<em>CITATION TO VERIFY: drought-induced tree mortality literature</em>].
						Understanding whether and how forests exploit these minor water pathways during stress has
						direct implications for models of forest vulnerability and resilience, and for silvicultural
						management strategies under projected future drought regimes.
					</p>
				</section>

				<!-- RESEARCH QUESTIONS -->
				<section>
					<h2>2. Research Questions</h2>
					<div class="rq-grid">
						<div class="rq-card">
							<span class="rq-label">RQ1</span>
							<p>
								To what extent do <strong>foliar water uptake</strong> and <strong>forest floor
								evaporation</strong> contribute to the total water balance of a forest stand during
								drought events, and how does the magnitude and timing of these contributions vary
								across the three experimental sites in Luxembourg, Switzerland, and the Netherlands?
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="rq-card">
							<span class="rq-label">RQ2</span>
							<p>
								Can <strong>stable isotopic tracers</strong> (δ¹⁸O, δ²H) reliably separate foliar
								water uptake and forest floor evaporation from the dominant transpiration signal,
								and what are the methodological limits of the <strong>two water worlds
								framework</strong> when applied to these minor pathways under drought conditions?
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="rq-card">
							<span class="rq-label">RQ3</span>
							<p>
								How can quantitative estimates of these minor water fluxes be integrated into a
								<strong>stand-scale ecohydrological model</strong>, and what do the resulting
								process constraints imply for the simulated resilience of forest water budgets
								to drought?
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<!-- WORK PACKAGES -->
				<section>
					<h2>3. Work Packages</h2>
					<div class="wp-list">
						<div class="wp-item">
							<div class="wp-header">
								<span class="wp-badge">WP1</span>
								<h3>Field Instrumentation and Multi-site Data Collection</h3>
							</div>
							<p>
								Deployment and maintenance of micrometeorological monitoring stations, isotopic
								sampling networks (precipitation, throughfall, soil water at multiple depths, xylem
								water), and sap flow sensors across the three experimental sites. By the end of
								Year 1 this work package delivers a uniquely comprehensive, harmonised multi-site
								dataset as a standalone scientific output, publishable as a data or methods paper
								regardless of downstream modelling outcomes, and providing the observational
								backbone that all subsequent work packages depend on.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="wp-item">
							<div class="wp-header">
								<span class="wp-badge">WP2</span>
								<h3>Isotopic Analysis and Water Source Separation</h3>
							</div>
							<p>
								Laboratory analysis of δ¹⁸O and δ²H ratios from field samples, and application of
								isotopic mixing models implemented in a Bayesian framework to propagate sampling
								and analytical uncertainty through to source proportion estimates. This work
								package also evaluates the performance and limitations of the two water worlds
								framework for distinguishing foliar uptake and forest floor contributions from the
								dominant transpiration signal.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="wp-item">
							<div class="wp-header">
								<span class="wp-badge">WP3</span>
								<h3>Energetic Quantification of Forest Floor Evaporation</h3>
							</div>
							<p>
								Measurement of forest floor evaporation using sub-canopy eddy covariance and
								Bowen ratio energy balance methods. Cross-validation between the energy balance
								approach and the isotopic mass balance from WP2 provides methodological
								redundancy: agreement between the two independent methods validates both;
								divergence is itself informative about systematic biases in sub-canopy turbulence
								measurement or isotopic mixing assumptions.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="wp-item">
							<div class="wp-header">
								<span class="wp-badge">WP4</span>
								<h3>Integration into a Stand-Scale Ecohydrological Model</h3>
							</div>
							<p>
								Development of process representations of foliar water uptake and forest floor
								evaporation within a stand-scale ecohydrological modelling framework. The model
								will be calibrated and evaluated against the multi-site observational dataset from
								WP1–3, with particular attention to its ability to reproduce observed isotopic
								signatures and micrometeorological flux partitioning under varying drought
								intensities.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="wp-item">
							<div class="wp-header">
								<span class="wp-badge">WP5</span>
								<h3>Inter-site Synthesis and Forest Resilience Implications</h3>
							</div>
							<p>
								Comparative analysis across Luxembourg, Switzerland, and the Netherlands to
								identify the climate, species composition, and soil factors that govern the
								relative importance of foliar uptake and forest floor evaporation during drought.
								Synthesis of implications for forest drought resilience modelling and silvicultural
								management, including a contribution to the ForFuS interdisciplinary doctoral
								training programme's cross-disciplinary outputs.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<!-- TIMELINE -->
				<section>
					<h2>4. Project Timeline (48 months)</h2>
					<div class="timeline-table">
						<div class="tl-header">
							<span>Work Package</span>
							<span class="tl-yr">Year 1</span>
							<span class="tl-yr">Year 2</span>
							<span class="tl-yr">Year 3</span>
							<span class="tl-yr">Year 4</span>
						</div>
						{[
							{ wp: 'WP1: Field Data', y1: 100, y2: 50, y3: 20, y4: 0, note: 'Multi-site instrumentation operational by month 6' },
							{ wp: 'WP2: Isotopic Analysis', y1: 60, y2: 100, y3: 60, y4: 20, note: 'Continuous sampling throughout field campaign' },
							{ wp: 'WP3: Floor Evap.', y1: 40, y2: 100, y3: 60, y4: 10, note: 'Sub-canopy EC in parallel with WP2' },
							{ wp: 'WP4: Modelling', y1: 0, y2: 40, y3: 100, y4: 40, note: 'Model development once observational baseline established' },
							{ wp: 'WP5: Synthesis', y1: 0, y2: 0, y3: 40, y4: 100, note: 'Inter-site synthesis and resilience implications' },
						].map(({ wp, y1, y2, y3, y4 }) => (
							<div class="tl-row">
								<span class="tl-label">{wp}</span>
								<div class="tl-bar-cell">
									<div class="tl-bar" style={`width: ${y1}%`}></div>
								</div>
								<div class="tl-bar-cell">
									<div class="tl-bar" style={`width: ${y2}%`}></div>
								</div>
								<div class="tl-bar-cell">
									<div class="tl-bar" style={`width: ${y3}%`}></div>
								</div>
								<div class="tl-bar-cell">
									<div class="tl-bar" style={`width: ${y4}%`}></div>
								</div>
							</div>
						))}
					</div>
				</section>

				<!-- CHALLENGES -->
				<section>
					<h2>5. Expected Challenges and Mitigations</h2>
					<div class="challenge-grid">
						<div class="challenge-card">
							<h3>Energy Balance Closure</h3>
							<p class="challenge-desc">Sub-canopy eddy covariance residuals of 10–30% of net radiation can mask the forest floor evaporation signal entirely</p>
							<p>Cross-validation with the isotopic mass balance from WP2 provides an independent, physically distinct estimate of floor evaporation; convergence between the two methods validates the approach, divergence is itself a scientifically informative result</p>
						</div>
						<div class="challenge-card">
							<h3>Xylem Water Extraction</h3>
							<p class="challenge-desc">Cryogenic extraction of xylem water for isotopic analysis may introduce systematic bias from cellulose-bound water contamination</p>
							<p>Triangulation between cryogenic extraction, direct vapour equilibration, and published isotopic correction procedures; multi-method comparison on a subset of samples to quantify and correct extraction artefacts before bulk analysis</p>
						</div>
						<div class="challenge-card">
							<h3>Inter-site Heterogeneity</h3>
							<p class="challenge-desc">Differences in species composition, soil properties, and regional climate between Luxembourg, Switzerland, and the Netherlands limit direct comparability</p>
							<p>Standardised field measurement and sample processing protocols across all three sites; hierarchical statistical model treating site identity as a random effect to separate process-level relationships from site-specific intercepts</p>
						</div>
						<div class="challenge-card">
							<h3>Stand vs. Tree Scale</h3>
							<p class="challenge-desc">Within-stand variability in foliar water uptake and sap flow rates between trees of different size classes complicates stand-level upscaling</p>
							<p>Stratified sampling design based on diameter class and dominant species; geostatistical upscaling methods to estimate stand-level fluxes with explicit uncertainty bounds propagated from the tree-level measurements</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<!-- EXPECTED OUTPUTS -->
				<section>
					<h2>6. Expected Outputs</h2>
					<ul class="outputs-list">
						<li>First-author papers in peer-reviewed ecohydrology journals, targeting outlets including <em>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</em>, <em>Agricultural and Forest Meteorology</em>, and <em>Ecohydrology</em></li>
						<li>An open-access multi-site dataset of stable water isotope ratios (δ¹⁸O, δ²H) and sub-canopy micrometeorological fluxes from the Luxembourg, Switzerland, and Netherlands experimental sites</li>
						<li>A methodological and scientific contribution to the ForFuS interdisciplinary doctoral training unit's cross-disciplinary programme and its network of partner institutions</li>
					</ul>
				</section>

				<!-- BACKGROUND -->
				<section>
					<h2>7. Background and Added Value</h2>
					<p>
						My M.Sc. thesis at Politecnico di Milano (building, calibrating, and running sensitivity
						and uncertainty analysis on the FEST process-based hydrological model over the Po basin)
						gave me direct experience of the methodological tension this PhD addresses from the
						modelling side: constraining poorly identifiable sub-processes from aggregate or indirect
						observations. In the Po catchment, the individual contributions of surface runoff,
						interflow, and deep percolation cannot be measured directly at the spatial and temporal
						resolution needed for model calibration; they must be inferred from the integrated
						streamflow signal and from spatial patterns in soil moisture and groundwater levels. The
						challenge of separating foliar water uptake and forest floor evaporation from a bulk eddy
						covariance flux is structurally analogous; the solution strategy (multi-source
						observations, Bayesian uncertainty propagation, cross-validation between independent
						methods) is the same.
					</p>
					<p>
						My current role at Progesi S.p.A. as a Natural Risk Assessment Specialist involves the
						operational integration of data from heterogeneous sources (numerical weather prediction
						output from ICON-I2 and ECMWF-IFS, ground-based sensor networks, and satellite-derived
						products) under real-time constraints and with direct operational consequences.
						Coordinating a field monitoring network across three international sites, managing sample
						collection logistics, and quality-controlling multi-source timeseries data are operational
						demands for which this role has directly prepared me.
					</p>
					<p>
						Isotope hydrology and ecophysiological instrumentation (xylem water extraction
						techniques, sub-canopy eddy covariance system configuration, and sap flow measurement
						methods) are competences I will develop during the doctoral programme under Prof. Keim's
						supervision at LIST. I do not present them as existing skills. The quantitative
						contribution I bring to the project from day one is in process-based hydrological
						modelling, multi-source data integration, and uncertainty-aware calibration: a
						methodological foundation from which the field-specific techniques of isotope ecohydrology
						can be systematically acquired.
					</p>
				</section>

			</div>
		</main>

		<ContactCTA />
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

